## Context and Problem Statement

In a project like sfpowerscripts where the tool is evolving and changing over time it becomes important to maintain track of the releases (especially of a semantic nature).

## Decision

In the concept of a release, we have a notion of changes (semantic releases) and commits (git releases). This will be implemented with the help of a version.

In this case the release name is the version number, for example: 1.0.0, 1.1.0, 1.2.0 and so on.

Releases are numbered sequentially and monotonically.

All releases must contain at least a unique version number.

## Sample Data

Releases like:

| Release | Changes                                                                                      | Commit  |
| ------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |
| 0.1.0  | Add a new configuration setting called "settings/my-setting"                                 | *a hash*  |
| 0.2.0  | Update a few new features to a 1.0-inspired / first version. Update configuration setting to a new settings/my-setting. | *a hash*  |
| 0.3.0  | Update new feature with a 1.0-inspired / first version. Update configuration setting to a new settings/my-setting. | *a hash*  |

This releases will be used to distinguish:

-   the new feature release `0.2.0`
-   the initial release `0.3.0`
-   that is there only a single setting. 
-   that is there are different releases of this setting (which are distinguished by number 0.2.0 and 0.3.0 respectively)

## Sample Script

```
my-script.js
#!/usr/bin/env node

const sfpowerscripts = require('../sfpowerscripts');

module.exports.sfpowerscripts = sfpowerscripts;

module.scope.versions = function() {
    return this.releases;
}

module.scope.release = function(releaseNumber) {
    return this.releases[releaseNumber];
}

module.scope.isRelease = function(releaseNumber) {
    return releaseNumber >= 0 && releaseNumber < this.releases.length;
}

module.scope.releaseHistory = function() {
    return [...this.releases];
}

module.scope.releaseDetails = function(releaseNumber) {
    return this.releases[releaseNumber];
}
```

## Sample Configuration

```
config.js
{
    settings:{
        my-setting:{
            release:"0.3.0"
        }
    }
}
```

