## Context

In 7.1. the following was added to make it possible to link defects to the corresponding Jira ticket
```
defects {
  id: {
    // from Jira, will be empty, if no link exists yet
  },
  name: {
    // from Jira, will be empty, if no link exists yet
  },
  severity: {
    // from Jira, enum: 'MINOR/MODERATE/SERIOUS/CRITICAL'
  },
  description: {
    // from Jira, can be empty
  },
  ticket_url: {
    // from Jira, can be empty
  },
  created: {
    // from Jira, datetime object
  }
}
```

## Decision

The following is now available at API level for getting defect data
```
defects {
  with_ticket {
    id: {},
    name: {},
    severity: {},
    description: {},
    ticket_url: {},
    created: {}
  }
}
```

The change that will occur and the people affected by the change.

## Consequences

The effect of the change is the additional feature of showing defects which are linked to tickets.

## Other options

The alternative of the above is to make tickets show defects directly. The advantages of doing
it this way are:
- there is no additional API calls required to get the defects
- it's simpler to make the defects searchable within the tickets
- it's simpler to get the defects in other projects or from other teams (assuming they are stored in one defect project per project / team)

However, there are significant problems with this approach
- this might lead to showing defects in projects where they are not actually required. This is the consequence of the decision-making framework "always using best judgement" which is applied here by default
- it means the same project will contain defects with different severity levels
- defects now appear to be associated with projects and teams, where they are not actually required for them

