### Context
Managing many packages within a project is a painful and error prone process. For this reason, the creation of yarn workspaces was undertaken. This makes the creation, management, and deployment of complex applications easier.

### Decision
To make adoption of yarn workspaces as frictionless as possible, a script has been added to `build.js` that will create a yarn workspace and run the relevant build commands.

### Status
Accepted

### Consequences
Adopting this script allows us to remove a large amount of repetitive code from our existing build scripts. It makes it easier to have the different build steps be encapsulated in separate functions and makes it easier for developers to learn and understand our build process.

## Detailed Description
By default, `yarn install` will install all of the packages for an application (server and client). It is not possible to install only the client packages as these commands are run with `yarn deploy`. This is problematic as our client packages are not only used by the client but by the test framework (e.g. `yarn test`).

Because we use yarn workspaces, we can move the server code (the part used in tests) into a separate package (in this case, `node_modules/.bin`), run `yarn build --global` to create the new package, and deploy that package to the npm registry. The existing `index.js` will no longer export the `server` part of our code, instead we will export the `client` part.

## More Details
The `build.js` file has been added to the project such that it can be executed by the script `yarn build`. The file has been edited so it can be executed in nodejs. This has allowed us to remove the `yarn --cwd` from the `run-all-builds` command that is now also run by `yarn rebuild`. The script that runs all of the build commands (called `run-all-builds`) lives within `yarn-scripts.js`, which also run the tests.

