## Context

we wanted to make sure that the new page would only appear when using markdown and we need to decide how we will make that happen and where the code will live.

* we do not want to make the page disappear when switching from non-markdown to markdown
* we do not want to mix our rendering logic into the new page markup so that our code does not become tangled

### Approach 1: Use a function to detect markdown from a media attribute on the page element

We can pass this function into the markup and pass the page element to it and it will return true or false depending on the value of the media attribute.

```js
const myPage = document.querySelector(`div#wrapper .page`);
const markdownPage = myPage.getAttribute('data-markdown');
if (markdownPage === 'true') {
  //do our thing here
}
```

This allows our renderer to determine whether we should be applying the markdown effects. However, this doesn't work if we start using nested elements, because those elements won't have a data-markdown attribute. So we would need to cache each parent element and check the attribute on each element using the forEach() method on the DOM element.

This would be very un-performant, especially on an older page, and become very costly as the page grows and we add more logic for switching between markdown and not markdown pages.

### Approach 2: Use a function to detect markdown from a function call on the page element

We can pass this function into the markup and pass the page element to it and it will return true or false depending on the value of the function call.

```js
const myPage = document.querySelector(`div#wrapper .page`);
const markdownPage = myPage.call(myPage, 'is-markdown-page()');
if (markdownPage === 'true') {
  //do our thing here
}
```

This approach has the same problem as the previous approach when it comes to performance (it doesn't work with elements other than the page element). It is also problematic when we start using recursive parent elements because the page element cannot call the function for a parent page element because it doesn't have access to the DOM structure.

Instead of passing the method into the markup and using a function call to the page element, we can directly invoke the function on the page element once it is mounted (as long as the page has already been fully loaded).

```js
const myPage = document.querySelector(`div#wrapper .page`);
const markdownPage = myPage.isMarkdownPage();
if (markdownPage === 'true') {
  //do our thing here
}
```

This is much faster than the previous approaches and allows us to continue to use this approach for any future pages we make.

#### How does this work?

1. We set a callback function as a global variable (this is required by the method we are using)
2. We pass this callback function into the markup, which then returns this function to the rest of our code.
3. Later we call this function using the scope of the page element and use the return value to determine whether or not we should be applying the markdown effects.

### Approach 3: Use a data attribute on the page element that is set when the page is switched to Markdown

We can use a data attribute to store the value of the attribute in the page element and get it each time we use it.

```js
const myPage = document.querySelector(`div#wrapper .page`);
const markdownPage = myPage.data('markdown');
if (markdownPage === 'true') {
  //do our thing here
}
```

This is fast but it isn't really reusable for any future elements that we use, because our markup is hard coded for it. So any page that we create from the scratch using this approach will only work with this page.

#### How does this work?

1. We change the attribute to get the attribute value
2. We set the value to the data attribute for the page element

### Approach 4: Don't use attributes on the page element

We can keep a function to detect markdown from the markup and make calls to this function when we use the page element.

```js
const myPage = document.querySelector(`div#wrapper .page`);
const markdownPage = myPage.hasMarkdown();
if (markdownPage === 'true') {
  //do our thing here
}
```

