## Status

Accepted

## Context

Google Cloud Functions (https://cloud.google.com/functions/) have an
entrypoint config for entrypoint lambda functions.

This can be used in a `main.go` file of the cloud function to set the
function entrypoint with the following snippet:

```go
type __Handler struct{}

type __Handler interface {

	// __
	__

	Entrypoint() []string
}

type __Default struct{}

type __Default interface {}

type __CloudFunction
type __CloudFunction interface {
	Entrypoint() []string
}

func (f:__CloudFunction) Entrypoint() []string {}

func (f:__CloudFunction) Init() {}

func (f:__CloudFunction) Main() {}

func (f:__CloudFunction) Build() {}

func (f:__CloudFunction) Deploy() {}

func (f:__CloudFunction) Stop() {}

func (f:__CloudFunction) Update() {}

func (f:__CloudFunction) Delete() {}
```

And then, add an entrypoint in a build and deploy script. This is then
available for deployment as the entrypoint. The example above doesn't
have an entrypoint, but a serverless build script is now provided. It can
be run with `gcloud deploy --entrypoint '*' __func_name__`. The '*'
entrypoint stands in for the entrypoint, which allows you to discover
the entrypoint in code, e.g. this example.

```shell
gcloud deploy --entrypoint '*' __func_name__
```

## Decision

The decision is to use the entrypoint function, but it was decided to also
add an `init` method to the `__CloudFunction` interface so that the
entrypoint could be more explicitly set by the serverless.

`Init` executes before the `Main` function and therefore is executed
before the entrypoint is invoked.

## Consequences

With an `init` method now in the `__CloudFunction` interface, the
entrypoint could be more explicitly set by the serverless. This will
require a major version change for the cloud function, which will trigger a
redeploy and potentially a `build` (major version change) as well. It's
up to the serverless to decide when such a major change is made.

A consequence of adding an `init` method is also that `__func_name__` must
be replaced with a generic function name like `__main_func__` so that it
doesn't conflict with a `__func_name__` entrypoint.

`__func_name__` will be able to accept a function of the form
`__func_name__*__func_name__*` and therefore is not fully generic.

