## Status

Accepted

## Context

A need has been expressed for consistency when recording dates in academic articles. To increase this consistency a standard must be applied.

A standard format for dates should be a date format that is mostly culture-neutral. The most common date formats in the US include:

- mm/dd/yyyy
- yyyy-mm-dd

Dates presented to the database must be culture-neutral. A standard format should be preferred.

Dates must be displayed to readers of the article in a culture-neutral format. A standard format should be preferred.

## Decision

Dates are to be displayed using the ISO 8601 format: yyyy-mm-dd.

The date format is already used for storing dates in the database.

The date format is culture-neutral, and [is preferred][Wikipedia:date_format] by the [Internationalized Information for Libraries][IIIF].

The date format can be expressed using the following two Java classes:

```java
public abstract static class Dates {
    public static String fromUnix(long unixTimestamp) {
        return new Instant(unixTimestamp).format(DateTime.ISO_8601_DATE_TIME_FORMAT);
    }
}
```

```java
public class Instant {
    private final long time;

    public Instant(long time) {
        this.time = time;
    }

    public String format(String format) {
        return format.replaceAll("yyyy", year());
        // more formatting can be added here, such as mm/dd instead of dd, etc
    }

    private String year() {
        return Integer.toString(year());
    }

    private int year() {
        return time / 10000;
    }
}
```

[Wikipedia:date_format]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Date_format#Western_culture
[IIIF]: https://iiif.github.io/lang/en-iiif.html#sections.dates.standard-format

## Consequences

Consistency of dates is improved within the department and across articles.

Consistency can be maintained and the use of the standard format can be enforced using some form of linting.

Dates in articles are displayed to readers in a culture-neutral format.

Some [date formats have concerns][Wikipedia:date_format:concerns]. Because the use of the ISO 8601 date format is a standard, these concerns should not apply.

[Wikipedia:date_format:concerns]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Date_format#Western_culture_(concerns)

## Context
When the code is run with the test helper, e.g., `tests/readThenWrite.test.js`, a function `testHelper` is given as an argument.
In this function, `const input = "foobar";` should be replaced with `const input = "${data.input}";`.

## Decision
In order to support this, variable names are hardcoded with `const` so that in test files, the names with the wildcard have meaning.

For example, the test `tests/readThenWrite.test.js` has the following function:
```
function testHelper(data = {}) {
    const input = "foobar";

    ...
}
```
This is replaced with:
```
function testHelper(data = {}) {
    const input = "${data.input}";

    ...
}
```
The variable name `data` is hardcoded.

## Status
Under review

## Consequences
Hardcoded variables make future code changes difficult.

In general, it's preferrable not to hardcode variable names. This is
especially true with the advent of [test-driven development](https://www.jstorimer.com/test-driven-development.html).
Instead, variable names should be generated via factory functions.

Here is an example of hardcoded variables in a different file:
```js
// tests/integration/products.test.js
const productsRepo = require('../../../../app/repo/products');

module.exports.productsRepo = productsRepo;

function testHelper() {
    const products = productsRepo.all();

    // ....
}
```
This code works, but hardcodes the variable `products`. Better would be to pass in the data structure via a function and then refactor to return objects:
```js
function testHelper(data = {}) {
    const products = data.products;

    // ....
}
```

There are some issues here that hardcode `data` as well. This could be solved
by exporting the data structure via module functions instead:
```
module.exports.productsRepo = productsRepo;
module.exports.products = products;

function testHelper(data = {}) {
    const products = data.products;

    const productsRepo = products;

    // ....
}
```
At the point of exporting the data structure, its contents can change with time,
instead of being hardcoded.

