## Context
Our model has to allow the player to be able to move around in the game world, interact with objects and so on. This should be done through a common interface that all player related classes inherit from, like this:

```ts
interface Iplayer {
    //...
}

class IMyself implements Iplayer {
    //...
}

class IYou implements Iplayer {
    //...
}
```

This approach allows us to provide common methods for both of these types (in this example):

```ts
// shared methods
public start(): void;
public stop(): void;
public render(): void;
```

While `IMyself` and `IYou` will probably have their own methods specific to themselves, this approach allows us to provide the interface to "the player" and thus the client could access the same interface through these two classes. We could have a global `Iplayer` type as well, but this makes the inheritance tricky, because it's not clear if it's intended for the player to inherit from the "base" player type or not. It would also be difficult to handle methods that are specific to the player or specific to the two main player types.

## Decision
For now, we will use this interface to define the interface of the player. Since this will be mostly a base interface, we will probably use a concrete class, IMyself, to inherit from and then create IYou as a class that inherits from IMyself. It's possible to add the Iplayer interface into IYou so that it's more readable, but I believe this is the wrong approach. This class should be used only for the two main player types, because we don't want to create classes that have to inherit from these two classes, like the `IMyRobot`, etc.

We also don't want to treat the classes like IMyRobot and IYou as the only types that can implement this interface. This class should be used only by player-oriented classes. We probably can't get away with having the method `start` used by a class that isn't a player-oriented class. This leads to a very constrained interface, which isn't necessarily a good thing, though it probably makes sense to use the start/stop/render methods only by player-oriented classes.

