## Context

For the data pipeline, we will require a way to identify which hashes are valid for which organisations.

The current system for doing this is the "event hash": 

- The first 12 bits of the hash contain the number, e.g. "0000". This identifies which organisation.
- The last 12 bits of the hash contain the ID of the event, e.g. "000000". This identifies which "thing" (event) within an organisation.

We'd like to make the system easier to understand for people who aren't familiar with the system, and to make it easier for the system to detect errors. We could do this by introducing a third number into the hash, in the last 16 bits.

However, this would break the current system (since 0-99999999 is not valid as an ID). It also means we can no longer encode a full number in the hash. In practice, we only need the last 3-4 digits for an ID. However, it means we can no longer encode a full number in the hash. For instance, 9999 becomes 0999, which is ambiguous. 

We can still encode the last 3-4 digits in the ID (the "units digit") in the hash. And we could encode 0 as a special bit in the last 3-4 digits, so the value 0-9999 could be fully encoded using the last 3-4 digits of the ID.

