## **Context**

**A. We need to define a way for publishing the routes for the pages we want to add to the Navigation Bar for the new apps we create.** 

- We want to add new routes to existing apps, not just apps we've created ourselves.
- We should be able to add routes to existing apps when a page is added to the Navigation Bar.

**B. Angular apps have their own routing module.**

- By default Angular apps come with a pre-defined route, called the 'root' route, which represents the home page.
- Angular apps have a routes object in the 'apps' folder. This object has two types, the routes and the router routes.

**C. Angular routing is tied to the DOM.**

- In order to enable routing, we need to add anchor elements (`<a>`) to the DOM that correspond to the route paths.
- This means that, by default, no apps can be hosted on other paths other than `/`.

**D. Angular apps can't have nested routes.**

- Angular apps can be added to other routes, but not nested under them.

**E. Angular apps can only have one main route.**

- Angular apps can only have one main route, which is the root route that represents the home page.
- Other routes can be added to Angular apps, but this is done via a custom route so the other route is only available inside the app that it has been added to.

## **Decision**

**Define a way to publish routes for the pages we want to add to the Navigation Bar for the new apps we create, so we can add routes to the Navigation Bar for existing apps and existing pages.**

- Add a new variable to the Repository Angular App object to define the custom routes we want to add to the Navigation Bar for the new apps we create.
- We will add these custom routes to the root route in the 'routing.module.ts' file in the Repository Angular App. This means that all apps created with the Repository Angular App will have the custom routes in their routes objects.
- We will add anchor elements (`<a>`) to the DOM for these custom routes so that they work as links. These elements will be added to the 'index.html' file by the Repository Web Components App.
- We will add additional route definitions to the root route in the 'routing.module.ts' file in the Repository Angular App that cover everything else. These routes won't be available for any of the custom routes.

